/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Ancient & Medieval (batch z) */
WB.L("rosetta-stone", "ancient", "beginner", "The Rosetta Stone: cracking the code of the pharaohs",
  "For fourteen centuries nobody on Earth could read an Egyptian hieroglyph. Then a broken slab of stone and a stubborn young Frenchman changed everything.",
  [
    ["A slab in a wall", "The Rosetta Stone carried one decree in three scripts, so readable Greek could unlock the unreadable Egyptian.",
      "In July 1799, during Napoleon’s invasion of Egypt, French soldiers rebuilding a fort near Rashid (Rosetta) in the Nile Delta noticed a dark block covered in writing. It was a broken slab of granodiorite, a little over a metre tall, with three bands of text.\n\nThe text was an ordinary decree from priests at Memphis in 196 BC, praising the young king Ptolemy V. What mattered was the format: hieroglyphs, Demotic (the everyday cursive script) and ancient Greek. Scholars could read Greek, so it could act as a crib for the other two. The British took the stone in 1801 under the terms of surrender, and it has been in the British Museum since 1802."],
    ["Why nobody could read it", "Hieroglyphs stayed unreadable for centuries because scholars wrongly assumed they were mystical symbols, not sounds.",
      "Hieroglyphs were used for over three thousand years, but as Egypt became part of the Greek, Roman and Christian world, fewer people learnt them. The last known inscription was carved at the temple of Philae in AD 394, and the knowledge vanished.\n\nFor centuries, European scholars believed each picture stood for a deep idea. In the seventeenth century, Athanasius Kircher published elaborate translations that were pure invention. That assumption was the real barrier. While people looked for hidden philosophy in every owl and reed, they could not see that many signs simply spelt out sounds, like letters. Even with the Greek crib, cracking the code took more than twenty years."],
    ["Names in ovals", "Matching royal names in oval cartouches to the Greek text revealed that hieroglyphs could spell sounds.",
      "Some hieroglyphs were enclosed in an oval ring, now called a cartouche. Scholars suspected these held royal names. In the 1810s, the English polymath Thomas Young, a physicist and doctor, showed that one cartouche matched the name Ptolemy in the Greek text, and he identified several sounds correctly. But he believed sound signs were used mainly for foreign names.\n\nThen a second inscription helped. An obelisk from Philae carried the names Ptolemy and Cleopatra, which share the letters p, t, o and l. Where the same signs spelt the shared sounds in both cartouches, even in different positions, that was strong evidence of an alphabet-like system."],
    ["Champollion’s leap", "Champollion proved in 1822 that sound signs were used throughout the script, even for native Egyptian names.",
      "Jean-François Champollion, a French linguist, had studied languages obsessively since boyhood, including Coptic, the late form of Egyptian still used in Christian church services. In 1822, he studied cartouches copied from temples such as Abu Simbel. One began with a sun disc. In Coptic, the word for sun was ‘re’. It ended with signs he already read as ‘s’, and between them was a sign he linked to the Coptic word for birth, ‘mise’.\n\nTogether they spelt Ramesses, a truly Egyptian pharaoh, not a Greek one. Sound signs were used throughout the script. A family story says he rushed to his brother’s office, cried that he had done it, and collapsed."],
    ["How the script works", "Hieroglyphs mix sound signs and whole-word signs, with silent determinatives and no written vowels.",
      "Champollion announced his results in a famous letter in September 1822, and the decades that followed confirmed and refined them. Hieroglyphic writing proved to be a clever mixture. Some signs stand for a single consonant, some for two or three consonants together, and some for a whole word.\n\nMany words end with a silent sign called a determinative, which tells the reader what kind of thing is meant. A pair of walking legs might mark a verb of movement, while a seated man marks a person. Vowels were usually not written, which is why scholars still argue over how names were pronounced. Once the system was understood, temple walls, tomb paintings and papyri could finally speak again."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was the Rosetta Stone so valuable to scholars?", ["It contained a long history of the pharaohs","It was the oldest known example of hieroglyphs","One decree appeared in Greek, which they could read","It explained the rules of Egyptian grammar directly"], 2, "Because the three texts said the same thing, the known Greek could serve as a crib for the other two scripts."],
    [1, "What was the main barrier to reading hieroglyphs for centuries?", ["Scholars took the signs for mystical ideas, not sounds","No example of the script survived anywhere in Egypt","The signs were too worn and faint to copy accurately","Greek scholars refused to study the Egyptian script"], 0, "Looking for hidden philosophy in every picture stopped people noticing that many signs spelt out sounds."],
    [2, "What did comparing the Ptolemy and Cleopatra cartouches suggest?", ["Cartouches were reserved for the names of gods only","Every sign in the script stood for a whole word","Greek names were written in a completely separate script","The script had an alphabet-like system of sound signs"], 3, "The same signs for p, t, o and l turned up in both names, which pointed to signs for sounds."],
    [3, "Why did the name Ramesses matter for Champollion’s breakthrough?", ["It proved the Greek text was a faithful translation","It showed sound signs spelt native Egyptian names too","It was the first name found on the stone itself","It showed Coptic had nothing to do with Egyptian"], 1, "A purely Egyptian name spelt with sound signs showed the principle applied throughout the script, not just to foreign names."],
    [4, "What does a determinative do in hieroglyphic writing?", ["It marks the vowels that the writer left unwritten","It stands for two or three consonants at once","It is a silent sign hinting at a word’s meaning","It marks the end of a royal name in a cartouche"], 2, "Determinatives give meaning clues, such as a seated man for a person, without being pronounced."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"For fourteen centuries no one could read Egyptian. A broken slab changed that."});

WB.L("norman-conquest-1066", "ancient", "beginner", "1066: the year England changed hands",
  "In a single autumn, England's king fought two invasions, marched hundreds of kilometres and died on a ridge in Sussex. The country that came out the other side would never be the same.",
  [
    ["Three claimants, one crown", "When Edward the Confessor died childless in 1066, three men claimed the English throne.",
      "Edward the Confessor died on 5 January 1066 without children and with no obvious heir. The English council quickly chose Harold Godwinson, the richest and most powerful earl in the kingdom, and he was crowned the very next day.\n\nTwo other men believed the crown was theirs. William, Duke of Normandy, a distant relative of Edward, claimed that Edward had promised him the throne and that Harold had sworn an oath to support him. Across the North Sea, Harald Hardrada, King of Norway and one of the most famous warriors of his age, claimed it through an older agreement between earlier kings. Both began preparing invasions, and Harold had to guard a long coastline without knowing who would strike first."],
    ["Two invasions in one month", "Harold beat the Norwegians in the north, but three days later William landed in the south.",
      "Harold spent the summer waiting for William on the south coast, but the Normans were held in port by unfavourable winds. In September, with supplies low, Harold sent much of his army home.\n\nAlmost immediately, Harald Hardrada landed in the north with a large fleet, joined by Harold’s exiled brother, Tostig. They defeated the northern earls at Fulford on 20 September. Harold marched north astonishingly fast and surprised the Norwegians at Stamford Bridge on 25 September. It was a crushing victory: Hardrada and Tostig were both killed, and the age of great Viking invasions of England is often said to end there. Three days later, on 28 September, William’s army landed at Pevensey in Sussex."],
    ["The battle on the ridge", "At Hastings the English shield wall held all day, until the line thinned and Harold fell.",
      "Harold raced south again and took up a strong position on a ridge about ten kilometres from Hastings, where the town of Battle stands today. His soldiers formed a shield wall, a dense line of overlapping shields bristling with spears and long-handled Dane axes. William’s army combined archers, heavy infantry and mounted knights.\n\nAll day on 14 October the Normans attacked uphill, and all day the wall held. A rumour spread that William was dead, and he reportedly lifted his helmet to show his men his face. Norman accounts describe retreats, some panicked and some possibly feigned, that lured English soldiers off the ridge to be cut down. By evening Harold was dead, along with his brothers Gyrth and Leofwine, and the remaining army collapsed."],
    ["Not really a tapestry", "The Bayeux Tapestry is an embroidery that tells the story of 1066, and what it shows of Harold’s death is debated.",
      "The Bayeux Tapestry is not technically a tapestry. Tapestries are woven; this is embroidery, with coloured woollen threads stitched onto strips of linen. It is around seventy metres long and half a metre tall, and tells the story like a comic strip. It was likely commissioned by Bishop Odo, William’s half-brother, and many historians think English embroiderers made it. It shows over six hundred people, horses, ships, Halley’s Comet and a few rude figures in the margins. Its final section is lost.\n\nThe famous image of Harold’s death, a figure grasping an arrow near his eye, is debated. It may be Harold, or the man beside him cut down by a knight, or both. The tapestry was also restored in the nineteenth century, which may have altered the arrow."],
    ["What changed", "The Conquest replaced England’s ruling class, reshaped its landscape and left its mark on the English language.",
      "William was crowned in Westminster Abbey on Christmas Day 1066, but conquest took years. Rebellions in the north were met with the Harrying of the North in 1069 and 1070, a campaign of deliberate destruction that caused famine across a huge region.\n\nWithin a generation, almost every great English landholder had been replaced by a Norman or other Frenchman. Castles of earth and timber, then stone, appeared across the country, including the Tower of London. At Christmas 1085, William ordered the Domesday Book, an extraordinary survey of who owned what across most of England. French became the language of the court for centuries, which is why English has paired words: cow and beef, pig and pork, sheep and mutton."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was the English throne contested in 1066?", ["Harold Godwinson had no support among the English council","The crown had already been promised to Norway by treaty","Edward died childless, and three men claimed the crown","Edward left a will naming William of Normandy king"], 2, "With no obvious heir, Harold, William and Hardrada each claimed the throne."],
    [1, "Why was Harold’s position so difficult in September 1066?", ["He faced a northern invasion, then William in the south","He had disbanded his whole army before Hardrada landed","William had already taken London by that point","His own brother Tostig was leading the Norman fleet"], 0, "Harold’s victory at Stamford Bridge came only days before William landed at Pevensey, leaving his army exhausted."],
    [2, "How did the Normans finally break the English line at Hastings?", ["A surprise night attack on the sleeping English camp","Heavy cavalry charged straight through the shield wall","William’s archers destroyed the line from afar","Retreats, some perhaps feigned, drew men off the ridge"], 3, "The shield wall held all day, but men lured downhill could be cut off, and the line slowly thinned."],
    [3, "Why is the Bayeux Tapestry not strictly a tapestry?", ["It is painted in dye on long strips of linen","It is embroidery on linen, not a woven cloth","It was made by monks in a Norman abbey","It is too short to count as a proper tapestry"], 1, "Tapestries are woven, while this work is stitched with woollen thread onto linen."],
    [4, "Why does English have pairs like cow and beef, or pig and pork?", ["Latin replaced English in all farming terms","Norman farmers invented new animal breeds","French became the court language for centuries","Domesday clerks renamed foods for tax records"], 2, "The Norman ruling class spoke French, so English kept both words, often one for the animal and one for the meat."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In one autumn, England’s king fought two invasions. How did it end?"});

WB.L("cleopatra", "ancient", "beginner", "Cleopatra: the last pharaoh",
  "She has been painted as a seductress for two thousand years, mostly by her enemies. The real Cleopatra was a scholar-queen fighting to keep her country alive.",
  [
    ["A Greek queen of Egypt", "Cleopatra belonged to a Greek-speaking dynasty founded by one of Alexander the Great’s generals.",
      "Cleopatra VII was born in 69 BC, but her family story begins with Alexander the Great. After Alexander died, one of his generals, Ptolemy, took control of Egypt and founded a dynasty. For nearly three hundred years the Ptolemies ruled from Alexandria, the glittering Greek city on the Mediterranean coast. They presented themselves to Egyptians as pharaohs, but they spoke Greek and often married their own siblings to keep power in the family.\n\nHere is a perspective worth sitting with: Cleopatra lived closer in time to the Moon landing than to the building of the Great Pyramid of Giza. To her, the pyramids were already ancient monuments, more than two thousand years old."],
    ["Brains before beauty", "Ancient sources describe Cleopatra as charming, multilingual and politically skilled, not as a famous beauty.",
      "Hollywood made Cleopatra a famous beauty, but the ancient evidence points elsewhere. The Greek writer Plutarch, who drew on earlier sources, said her beauty was not in itself incomparable, but her presence was captivating, her conversation charming and her voice delightful.\n\nHe reported that she spoke many languages and could talk to foreign envoys without interpreters. According to Plutarch, she was the first of her dynasty to bother learning Egyptian, the language of the people she ruled. Her coins show a strong hooked nose and a firm chin, though coin portraits were also political images. Educated in Alexandria, one of the great centres of learning in the ancient world, she ruled as a skilled, calculating politician."],
    ["Caesar and the rolled-up queen", "Cleopatra won back her throne by allying with Julius Caesar.",
      "Cleopatra came to power around 51 BC as co-ruler with her younger brother, Ptolemy XIII, but his advisers soon drove her out. She raised an army, and the siblings were at war when Julius Caesar arrived in Alexandria in 48 BC, chasing his Roman rival Pompey.\n\nCleopatra needed to reach Caesar without being caught by her brother’s men. Plutarch says she was smuggled into the palace by boat at dusk, hidden inside a bag for bedding, the origin of the later legend about a carpet. Caesar took her side. After months of fighting in the city, Ptolemy XIII drowned while fleeing, and Cleopatra was restored to the throne. She later bore a son, Ptolemy Caesar, nicknamed Caesarion."],
    ["Antony and Actium", "Cleopatra’s partnership with Mark Antony ended in defeat at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC.",
      "Cleopatra was in Rome when Caesar was assassinated in 44 BC, and she quickly returned to Egypt. Mark Antony now controlled Rome’s eastern provinces. In 41 BC he summoned her to Tarsus, in modern Turkey. Plutarch describes her arriving on a barge with a gilded stern, purple sails and silver oars, dressed as the goddess Aphrodite. Whatever the exact details, it was political theatre, and it worked. They became partners in power and in private, and she had three children with him.\n\nIn 34 BC, Antony handed territories to Cleopatra and her children, and his rival Octavian used this as proof that Antony had betrayed Rome to a foreign queen. At the naval Battle of Actium in 31 BC, their fleet was beaten."],
    ["Written by the winners", "Cleopatra’s enemies shaped her image, but she spent her reign trying to keep Egypt independent.",
      "A year after Actium, Octavian’s armies closed in on Alexandria. Antony, believing Cleopatra was already dead, stabbed himself. Cleopatra died in August 30 BC, aged thirty-nine, rather than be paraded through Rome as a captive. The famous story says an asp, smuggled to her in a basket of figs, bit her. Yet even Plutarch admitted nobody knew the truth, and some historians think poison is more likely. Caesarion was later killed on Octavian’s orders.\n\nEgypt became a Roman province, ending three thousand years of pharaohs. Because Octavian won, his version dominated, and Roman poets painted Cleopatra as a drunken, power-hungry foreigner. Historians today see a ruler who used money, diplomacy and alliances to keep Egypt independent. Her tomb has never been found."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the background of the dynasty Cleopatra belonged to?", ["Native Egyptian priests who seized power","Greek rulers descended from Alexander’s general","Roman governors appointed by the Senate","Phoenician merchants who ruled from Carthage"], 1, "The Ptolemies were Greek rulers who presented themselves to Egyptians as pharaohs."],
    [1, "What does the ancient evidence suggest was Cleopatra’s real strength?", ["Her reputation as the most beautiful woman alive","Her skill as a general leading armies in battle","Her habit of ruling without any allies","Her intelligence, charm and languages"], 3, "Plutarch stressed her captivating presence, conversation and languages rather than her looks."],
    [2, "Why did Cleopatra ally with Julius Caesar?", ["She needed help regaining the throne from her brother","He had promised to make Egypt a Roman province","Her brother had asked her to negotiate for him","She wanted Rome to protect Alexandria from invaders"], 0, "Ousted by her brother’s advisers, she gained a powerful ally, and Caesar took her side."],
    [3, "Why did Antony’s gift of territories hurt him politically?", ["It made Cleopatra the official co-ruler of Rome","It drained Egypt of its money and its soldiers","Octavian cast it as betraying Rome to Egypt","Antony’s own army refused to accept the gift"], 2, "Octavian turned the ceremony in Alexandria into propaganda against Antony in Rome."],
    [4, "Why is the popular image of Cleopatra so unreliable?", ["No ancient writer ever wrote anything about her","Octavian won, so his hostile version dominated","Her own writings were later proven to be forgeries","She was an invention of later playwrights"], 1, "The victors told the story, so later writers inherited a picture shaped by her enemies."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Cleopatra’s famous looks may be the least reliable part of her story."});

WB.L("medieval-knights", "ancient", "beginner", "The real life of a medieval knight",
  "Forget cranes lifting clanking men onto horses. Real knights were trained athletes, expensive to equip, and often far less chivalrous than the stories suggest.",
  [
    ["Page, squire, knight", "Knighthood was earned through years of service, usually starting as a page at around seven.",
      "Becoming a knight was not a matter of simply buying a sword. In the classic pattern, a boy from a noble family was sent at around seven to serve in another lord’s household as a page. He learnt manners, riding, basic weapon handling and how to serve at table.\n\nAt around fourteen he became a squire, attached to a knight whose armour he cleaned, whose horses he tended and whom he followed onto the battlefield. If he proved himself, he might be dubbed a knight around twenty-one, sometimes in a solemn ceremony and sometimes in a hurry on the eve of battle. Ages varied a great deal, but the idea was constant: knighthood was earned through years of service."],
    ["An expensive business", "A knight was costly military equipment, so lords supported knights with land.",
      "A knight was in many ways a very expensive piece of military equipment. He needed armour, a sword, a lance, a shield, squires and servants, and above all horses. The finest warhorses, called destriers, were specially bred and trained for the noise and violence of battle, and could cost many times the price of an ordinary horse. He also needed riding horses for travel and pack horses for baggage.\n\nThis is why knights were usually supported by land. In return for military service, a lord granted them estates whose peasants produced the income. The cost was so heavy that, by the 1200s, the English crown was ordering qualified landowners to become knights, and many paid fines to avoid it."],
    ["Armour and heraldry", "Armour was lighter than the myth suggests, and painted shield designs identified knights in battle.",
      "Films show knights hoisted onto horses by cranes, helpless if they fell. That is a myth, popularised in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. A complete suit of plate armour, at its peak in the 1400s, typically weighed twenty to twenty-five kilograms, less than many modern soldiers carry. Because the weight was spread across the body, a fit man could run, mount a horse and get up after a fall. The real cost was stamina: experiments with volunteers in replica armour found walking and running took roughly twice the energy.\n\nFull-face helmets from the twelfth century made knights hard to identify, so they painted bold designs on shields and on the cloth surcoats worn over armour, hence ‘coat of arms’. These became hereditary, tracked by heralds. Many modern logos still borrow the look."],
    ["Tournaments and fame", "Tournaments began as near-real war and became a route to fortune and fame.",
      "Tournaments started in the eleventh and twelfth centuries as something close to real war. Teams of knights charged each other across open countryside in a mêlée, capturing opponents and holding them for ransom. People were injured and killed, and the Church repeatedly condemned them.\n\nFor a skilled but landless knight, though, they were a path to fortune. William Marshal, an English knight of the late twelfth century, reportedly captured hundreds of opponents and rose to become one of the most powerful men in England. Later tournaments became more organised and theatrical, with jousting, in which two knights charged each other with lances, often separated by a barrier. Champions became celebrities, and spectators watched from decorated stands."],
    ["Chivalry: ideal and reality", "Chivalry mostly applied between knights, while ordinary people often suffered cruelty.",
      "Chivalry described an ideal knight: brave, loyal to his lord, generous, courteous and devoted to the Church. Poets and romances about King Arthur and his knights spread these values, and many knights took them seriously. Rules of conduct also had practical benefits, since a captured noble was usually worth more alive, for ransom, than dead.\n\nBut chivalry mostly applied between knights. Ordinary soldiers and peasants often received no such mercy. Medieval armies regularly practised the chevauchée, a raid that deliberately burned villages and crops to ruin an enemy’s economy. The gap between the ideal and reality was obvious even at the time, and many medieval writers criticised knights for their cruelty and greed."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did a boy typically become a knight in the classic pattern?", ["Inheriting the title automatically at birth","Years of service as a page, then a squire","Buying a sword and armour at around fourteen","Passing a written exam set by the king"], 1, "Knighthood was earned through long service in another lord’s household, not simply bought."],
    [1, "Why were knights usually supported with land?", ["Land was the only thing the crown could pay with","Peasants refused to serve under landless knights","Knights were forbidden to earn money any other way","Their armour, horses and servants were very costly"], 3, "Estates, worked by peasants, produced the income needed to pay for the knight’s equipment."],
    [2, "What does modern evidence show about knights in plate armour?", ["They could run, mount horses and rise after a fall","They needed cranes to be lifted onto horses","They could barely walk because of its weight","They wore it only for ceremonies and parades"], 0, "A suit weighed about twenty to twenty-five kilograms, spread across the body, so a fit man could move freely."],
    [3, "Why did tournaments appeal to a skilled but landless knight?", ["Winning gave automatic ownership of an estate","The Church paid the best fighters a yearly wage","Ransom from captured knights could bring wealth","They were the only route to being knighted"], 2, "Ransom from captured knights, as in William Marshal’s career, could make a fortune."],
    [4, "How did chivalry work in practice?", ["It protected every person in a war zone, whatever their rank","It mostly applied between knights, not to peasants","It forbade all raids on enemy farmland and villages","It was never taken seriously by any knight at all"], 1, "Courtesy was reserved for fellow nobles, while raids like the chevauchée devastated ordinary communities."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Medieval knights weren’t helpless in armour. What was the real cost?"});

WB.L("tutankhamun-tomb", "ancient", "beginner", "Tutankhamun: the tomb that time forgot",
  "He was a minor king who died as a teenager. That is exactly why his tomb survived, and why its discovery in 1922 stunned the world.",
  [
    ["The boy king", "Tutankhamun was a minor king who restored Egypt’s old gods and died at about eighteen or nineteen.",
      "Tutankhamun ruled Egypt for only about nine or ten years in the fourteenth century BC, coming to the throne at around eight or nine. His father was very probably Akhenaten, the pharaoh who abandoned Egypt’s traditional gods to worship a single sun disc, the Aten. The boy was originally named Tutankhaten, meaning living image of the Aten.\n\nEarly in his reign, guided by powerful advisers, the court restored the old gods, and his name was changed to Tutankhamun, honouring the god Amun. He died at around eighteen or nineteen. To later Egyptians, he was a footnote, and later kings even left his name off official lists."],
    ["Carter’s last season", "After years of fruitless digging, Howard Carter found Tutankhamun’s tomb in November 1922.",
      "By the early twentieth century, most archaeologists believed the Valley of the Kings had given up all its secrets. Howard Carter, an English archaeologist, disagreed. Small finds bearing Tutankhamun’s name convinced him that the king’s tomb was still out there. His sponsor, the fifth Earl of Carnarvon, was ready to stop paying by 1922, but Carter persuaded him to fund one final season.\n\nOn 4 November 1922, workers uncovered a stone step cut into the bedrock, leading to a sealed doorway. On 26 November, Carter made a small hole and held up a candle. Asked whether he could see anything, he later recalled replying that he could see wonderful things: gilded couches, statues, chests and chariots, in a room no one had entered for over three thousand years."],
    ["Robbed, but not emptied", "Robbers entered the tomb, but it was soon resealed and forgotten, so most of its contents survived.",
      "Tutankhamun’s tomb was not untouched. Evidence showed that robbers broke in at least once, and probably twice, soon after the burial. They took small, easily carried valuables such as oils and metal objects before officials found the break-in, tidied up and resealed it.\n\nThen luck intervened. Flood debris and the rubble and huts of workers digging later tombs, including that of Ramesses VI, buried the entrance, and it was forgotten. The tomb itself was small, possibly a private tomb adapted in a hurry after his unexpected death. Yet it held over five thousand objects, and recording, conserving and removing them took Carter and his team about a decade."],
    ["Gold, and much more", "Everyday objects in the tomb reveal as much about Egyptian royal life as the gold does.",
      "The best known treasure is the funerary mask, made of gold inlaid with glass and semi-precious stones and weighing about ten kilograms. The king’s body lay inside three nested coffins, the innermost of solid gold and weighing over a hundred kilograms, enclosed within a stone sarcophagus and four gilded wooden shrines.\n\nBut many of the most revealing objects were ordinary: chariots, bows, walking sticks, board games, linen underwear, sandals, jars of food and wine, and a lock of hair that belonged to his grandmother, Queen Tiye. There were even two tiny mummified daughters, stillborn babies buried with their father. Together these objects let historians see an Egyptian royal life in unusual detail."],
    ["The curse that wasn’t", "The pharaoh’s curse was a newspaper invention, and how Tutankhamun died is still unresolved.",
      "In April 1923, just months after the opening, Lord Carnarvon died in Cairo. A mosquito bite, which he had accidentally nicked while shaving, became infected. Newspapers competing for stories spread the idea of a pharaoh’s curse, and novelists such as Arthur Conan Doyle encouraged it.\n\nNo curse inscription was found in the tomb. Howard Carter, who spent the most time inside, lived until 1939. Later studies comparing people present at the opening with others found no sign that they died earlier. How Tutankhamun himself died is still debated. Scans and DNA studies have pointed to a broken leg, a foot deformity and evidence of malaria, but no single cause is agreed."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What changed in Egyptian religion early in Tutankhamun’s reign?", ["A single sun disc was made the only god","Amun was removed from official worship","The old gods were restored after Akhenaten","Egypt adopted gods from neighbouring lands"], 2, "His name changed from Tutankhaten to Tutankhamun as worship of Amun and the old gods returned."],
    [1, "What kept Carter searching despite years without results?", ["Small finds bearing the king’s name","A map left behind by an earlier explorer","Carnarvon’s promise of unlimited funds","Records placing the tomb under Ramesses VI"], 0, "Those small finds convinced him the king’s tomb was still in the Valley of the Kings."],
    [2, "Why did most of the treasure survive in the tomb?", ["Robbers never found the burial chamber at all","Priests removed the most valuable items first","It was hidden deep inside a great pyramid","The entrance was buried and forgotten"], 3, "Robbers took only small items, and later rubble covered the entrance, so the tomb stayed hidden for over three thousand years."],
    [3, "Why did ordinary objects in the tomb matter to historians?", ["They showed that the gold objects were all fakes","They revealed everyday royal life in unusual detail","They were worth more than all the gold items","They proved the tomb had never been entered by anyone"], 1, "Chariots, games, sandals and food jars show how an Egyptian king lived, not just how he was buried."],
    [4, "What does the evidence say about the pharaoh’s curse?", ["Carter and most of his team died within a year","A curse inscription was carved above the entrance","No curse was found, and no excess of early deaths","Carnarvon died of an ancient poison in the tomb"], 2, "Carnarvon died of an infection, Carter lived until 1939, and studies found no unusual early deaths."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A minor king’s tomb survived 3,000 years. Its obscurity was the secret."});

WB.L("hannibal-punic-wars", "ancient", "intermediate", "Hannibal: the general who crossed the Alps",
  "He marched elephants over the Alps and destroyed Roman armies one after another. So why did Rome, not Hannibal, win the war?",
  [
    ["Rome, Carthage and revenge", "Carthage’s Barca family wanted revenge after Rome won the First Punic War, and Hannibal inherited that aim.",
      "In the third century BC, Rome, a rising land power that controlled most of Italy, faced Carthage, a wealthy trading city founded by Phoenicians in what is now Tunisia. The Romans called Carthaginians Poeni, hence the Punic Wars. In the first, from 264 to 241 BC, Rome, which had little naval experience, built fleets almost from scratch and won. Carthage lost Sicily and paid a huge indemnity.\n\nHamilcar Barca then conquered territory in Spain. His son Hannibal grew up in military camps there. Ancient historians tell how the nine-year-old swore at an altar never to be a friend of Rome. He took command at about twenty-six in 221 BC, and in 218 BC the Second Punic War began."],
    ["Over the mountains", "Hannibal attacked overland through the Alps, arriving in Italy with a fraction of his army.",
      "Rome controlled the sea, so Hannibal attacked overland, which no one expected. In 218 BC he led his army from Spain across the Pyrenees, through southern Gaul and over the Rhône, with war elephants ferried on rafts. Late in the year, he crossed the Alps.\n\nThe journey took about fifteen days in the mountains, through snow, landslides and ambushes by local tribes. Polybius, who wrote within living memory and claimed to have walked the route, says Hannibal reached Italy with about twenty thousand infantry and six thousand cavalry, a fraction of the force that had left Spain. Most of the elephants died soon after. Which pass he used is still debated."],
    ["Cannae: the perfect trap", "At Cannae, Hannibal’s weak centre drew the Romans into an encirclement that destroyed their army.",
      "Hannibal defeated the Romans at the Trebia in 218 BC, then ambushed an army in fog beside Lake Trasimene in 217. Rome responded under Quintus Fabius Maximus, who avoided pitched battle and shadowed Hannibal, earning the nickname Cunctator, the delayer. Frustrated Romans abandoned that approach.\n\nIn 216 BC at Cannae, a huge Roman army attacked. Hannibal placed his weakest troops in the centre, which bent backwards under pressure as planned, drawing the Romans in. His heavy infantry then closed in from both sides while his cavalry struck from behind. The Romans were surrounded and slaughtered. Ancient sources give fifty thousand or more dead, and military academies still study the battle."],
    ["Why Rome didn’t fall", "Rome survived Cannae because its allies mostly stayed loyal and Hannibal lacked the means to take the city.",
      "After Cannae, many expected Rome to surrender. It did not. The Senate refused to discuss peace, raised new legions, and returned to Fabius’s careful strategy. Hannibal hoped Rome’s Italian allies would desert in large numbers. Some, including Capua, did, but most of central Italy stayed loyal and kept supplying men and money.\n\nHannibal lacked siege equipment and reinforcements to attack Rome, and Carthage sent him little help. He stayed in Italy about fifteen years, undefeated in major battle yet unable to finish the war. Meanwhile a brilliant young Roman general, Publius Cornelius Scipio, conquered Carthaginian Spain and invaded North Africa, forcing Carthage to recall Hannibal to defend his home city."],
    ["Zama, exile and a myth", "Scipio beat Hannibal at Zama in 202 BC, and Rome later destroyed Carthage, though not by sowing it with salt.",
      "In 202 BC, Scipio and Hannibal met at Zama in North Africa. Scipio had learnt from his enemy, opening lanes in his lines for Hannibal’s charging elephants to pass through harmlessly, and his cavalry won the day. Carthage surrendered, gave up its fleet and overseas empire, and paid heavy tribute. Scipio was honoured with the name Africanus.\n\nHannibal led Carthage’s government for a time, then fled into exile in the East, and around 183 BC took poison rather than be handed to the Romans. In the Third Punic War, Rome besieged and destroyed Carthage in 146 BC. The popular story that the Romans sowed the ground with salt, however, appears to be a modern invention."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the Barca family want another war with Rome?", ["Rome had destroyed Carthage’s capital city","Carthage lost Sicily and paid a huge indemnity","Carthage had been denied access to Spain","Rome had killed Hannibal’s father in battle"], 1, "Defeat in the First Punic War left Carthage humiliated, which fuelled the desire for revenge."],
    [1, "Why was Hannibal’s route over the Alps surprising?", ["It was the shortest road from Spain to Rome","Roman armies had been defending the passes","The Alps were not yet thought to be difficult","Rome held the sea and expected no attack overland"], 3, "Since Rome held the sea, an attack through the Alps was unexpected, though it cost him much of his army."],
    [2, "How did Hannibal win at Cannae?", ["His centre gave way and the Romans were encircled","He attacked the Roman camp in a night raid","His elephants broke through the Roman centre","He ambushed the Roman army in a foggy valley"], 0, "The weak centre bent back as planned, and infantry and cavalry closed around the Romans."],
    [3, "Why did Rome survive after Cannae?", ["Carthage signed a peace treaty immediately","The Alps blocked Hannibal’s route of retreat","Most allies stayed loyal and Hannibal lacked help","Rome had beaten Hannibal’s army soon after"], 2, "Rome refused peace, its allies kept supplying men, and Hannibal could not attack the city itself."],
    [4, "What does the story of salting Carthage’s fields show?", ["Rome systematically poisoned all conquered farmland","Appealing stories can spread without ancient evidence","Carthage’s soil was naturally too salty to farm","Scipio ordered the fields salted straight after Zama"], 1, "The salt story appears to be a modern invention, not something the ancient sources record."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Hannibal won battle after battle yet lost the war. What went wrong?"});

WB.L("joan-of-arc", "ancient", "intermediate", "Joan of Arc and the Hundred Years' War",
  "A teenage peasant girl from a village in eastern France told a prince that God had sent her to save his kingdom. Astonishingly, she very nearly did.",
  [
    ["A very long war", "By the 1420s France was close to defeat, with English and Burgundian forces holding Paris and the north.",
      "The Hundred Years' War actually lasted 116 years, from 1337 to 1453, with long truces. At its heart was inheritance: English kings, descended from French nobility, held lands in France and claimed the French crown itself. England won famous victories at Crécy in 1346, Poitiers in 1356 and Agincourt in 1415, where Henry V’s outnumbered army, with its longbowmen, defeated a much larger French force.\n\nBy the 1420s, France was in desperate trouble. The Duke of Burgundy had sided with England, and the Treaty of Troyes in 1420 declared that the English king would inherit the throne, disinheriting the French heir, Charles, known as the Dauphin. Paris and much of the north were under English and Burgundian control."],
    ["Voices and a test", "Joan, an illiterate farmer’s daughter, convinced the cautious Dauphin by passing weeks of questioning.",
      "Joan was born around 1412 in Domrémy, in north-eastern France, to a farming family. She could not read or write. From about thirteen, she later said, she heard voices and saw saints, especially Michael, Catherine and Margaret, who told her to go to the Dauphin, drive out the English and see him crowned.\n\nIn early 1429, at around seventeen, she persuaded a local garrison commander to give her an escort, cut her hair short and rode in men’s clothing to the Dauphin’s court at Chinon. Charles was cautious: she could be a saint, a fraud or an agent of the devil. Churchmen at Poitiers questioned her for weeks and found nothing wrong in her faith or conduct. The situation was desperate enough to take a chance."],
    ["Orléans and the coronation", "Joan’s presence broke the siege of Orléans and led to Charles being crowned at Reims.",
      "Orléans, the key to southern France, had been under English siege since late 1428. Charles gave Joan armour, a banner and a place with an army bringing supplies. She arrived at the end of April 1429. She did not formally command, but her presence electrified soldiers and townspeople, and she pushed for bold attacks. She was wounded by an arrow between neck and shoulder but returned to the fight. On 8 May the English abandoned the siege.\n\nMore victories followed, including Patay in June. Joan then urged Charles to make the dangerous journey to Reims, the traditional site where French kings were crowned. On 17 July 1429 he was crowned Charles VII, with Joan standing nearby holding her banner."],
    ["Captured and tried", "Joan was captured in 1430, abandoned by Charles and tried for heresy by a Church court allied with the English.",
      "After the coronation, Joan’s fortunes turned. An attack on Paris failed, and the king grew more interested in negotiation than war. In May 1430, while defending Compiègne, she was pulled from her horse and captured by Burgundian soldiers, who sold her to the English. There is no evidence that Charles made any serious attempt to rescue her.\n\nIn 1431 she was tried for heresy in Rouen by a Church court led by Pierre Cauchon, a bishop allied with the English. They needed her condemned, since that would suggest Charles had been crowned with the devil’s help. The records show an illiterate young woman answering trick questions from learned men with remarkable sharpness and courage."],
    ["Burned, then vindicated", "Joan was executed in 1431, but a retrial cleared her in 1456 and she became a saint in 1920.",
      "One major charge was that she wore men’s clothing, which her accusers treated as a sin against divine law. Under pressure she signed a statement renouncing her claims and agreed to wear women’s dress, but days later she resumed men’s clothing, and the court declared her a relapsed heretic. On 30 May 1431, in the market square of Rouen, she was burned at the stake. She was about nineteen.\n\nWithin a generation, the war she had helped turn ended, as French armies took back Normandy and Gascony. By 1453, England held only Calais. A retrial authorised by the Pope declared the original verdict null in 1456. Joan was made a saint in 1920 and is a patron saint of France."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Treaty of Troyes in 1420 declare?", ["France would be divided between Burgundy and England","The Dauphin would be crowned king at Reims","The English king would inherit the French throne","The war would end in a lasting peace treaty"], 2, "It disinherited the Dauphin Charles, leaving France in desperate trouble."],
    [1, "Why did Charles have Joan examined before trusting her?", ["She could be a saint, a fraud or a danger","Church law banned women from joining armies","Burgundy had warned him that she was a spy","He had already decided she was a heretic"], 0, "Backing the wrong claimant would be disastrous, so churchmen tested her faith and conduct for weeks."],
    [2, "What was Joan’s main effect at Orléans?", ["She formally commanded the whole French army","She negotiated a truce with the English","She ended the siege by diplomacy alone","Her presence drove the army to bold attacks"], 3, "Though not in formal command, she pushed commanders to attack, and the English left on 8 May 1429."],
    [3, "Why did the English want Joan condemned as a heretic?", ["It would persuade Burgundy to join their side","It would suggest the devil had helped crown Charles","It would stop her from raising another army","It would let them retake the city of Orléans"], 1, "Discrediting Joan undermined the legitimacy of Charles’s coronation."],
    [4, "What happened after Joan’s execution?", ["Charles was never recognised as king","England kept all of its French lands","A retrial cleared her, and she became a saint","The Pope fully upheld the original verdict"], 2, "A retrial overturned the 1431 verdict in 1456, and she was canonised in 1920."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A teenage farm girl turned a lost war. What happened next surprises many."});

WB.L("hadrians-wall", "ancient", "intermediate", "Hadrian's Wall: life on the edge of an empire",
  "For nearly three hundred years, soldiers from across the Roman world guarded a stone wall in the rain of northern Britain. The letters they left behind are astonishingly human.",
  [
    ["The emperor’s frontier", "Hadrian chose to consolidate the empire, and his soldiers built a fortified wall of milecastles, forts and ditches across northern Britain.",
      "Hadrian became emperor in AD 117, when the empire was at its largest. Rather than keep conquering, he consolidated, and around AD 122 he visited Britain. Construction began soon after. The wall ran about 118 kilometres, from Wallsend on the Tyne to Bowness-on-Solway, built mainly by the three legions stationed in Britain.\n\nIt was more than a line of stone. The eastern part was stone up to about three metres thick; the western section began as turf and was later rebuilt in stone. Every Roman mile (about 1.5 kilometres) stood a small fort called a milecastle with a gateway, and two turrets lay between each pair. A ditch ran in front, an earthwork called the vallum behind, and around fifteen larger forts, such as Housesteads, were added."],
    ["What was it for?", "The wall was probably less a barrier against invasion than a way to control movement, tax trade and display Roman power.",
      "It is tempting to see the wall as a barrier against invading hordes. Hadrian’s biography, written centuries later, says he built it to separate the Romans from the barbarians. But most historians think its purpose was more complicated. Soldiers did not usually fight from the top of walls; they fought in the open, and the many gateways suggest people were expected to pass through, under supervision.\n\nIts main jobs were probably controlling who crossed, collecting customs duties, preventing small-scale raids and cattle rustling, and watching the tribes to the north. It was also a statement: a massive stone barrier from coast to coast announced the power of Rome. Historians still debate how much each purpose mattered."],
    ["Soldiers from everywhere", "Auxiliary troops recruited from across the empire garrisoned the wall, making the frontier remarkably multicultural.",
      "The legionaries who built the wall mostly moved elsewhere once it was finished. It was garrisoned by auxiliaries, soldiers recruited from the empire’s provinces who became Roman citizens after their service. Inscriptions show units from what are now Belgium, the Netherlands, Germany, Spain and beyond. At Arbeia, in modern South Shields, there was even a unit of bargemen from the Tigris river in Mesopotamia.\n\nA tombstone found there was set up by Barates, a man from Palmyra in Syria, for his wife Regina, a freed slave from the British Catuvellauni tribe. It carries an inscription in Latin and a line in Palmyrene Aramaic. The northern frontier was one of the most multicultural places in Roman Britain."],
    ["Postcards from Vindolanda", "The Vindolanda tablets are wooden letters and records that bring the everyday lives of Roman soldiers to life.",
      "Just south of the wall lies Vindolanda, a fort that predates Hadrian’s Wall. From 1973, archaeologists there led by Robin Birley began finding thin wooden tablets, preserved in waterlogged, oxygen-free soil, covered in ink handwriting from around AD 100. They include duty reports, supply lists and private letters.\n\nOne soldier is sent socks, sandals and two pairs of underpants. Another note dismissively calls the locals Brittunculi, little Britons. The most famous is a birthday invitation from Claudia Severa to her friend Sulpicia Lepidina, the wife of the fort’s commander. A scribe wrote most of it, but a few warm lines at the end appear to be in Claudia’s own hand, among the earliest known examples of Latin handwriting by a woman."],
    ["The wall today", "Hadrian’s Wall does not mark the England–Scotland border; it lies wholly within England.",
      "A common myth is that Hadrian’s Wall marks the border between England and Scotland. It does not. The whole wall lies within England, and in places the modern border is up to about a hundred kilometres to the north.\n\nAround AD 142, Hadrian’s successor Antoninus Pius pushed further into what is now Scotland and built the turf Antonine Wall, but it was abandoned within about twenty years and the army returned to Hadrian’s line. The wall was garrisoned until Roman rule in Britain ended in the early fifth century. Afterwards, much of its stone was taken to build farms, churches and roads. Even so, long stretches still stand, and since 1987 it has been listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is Hadrian’s Wall best described as?", ["A single stone barrier with no gates at all","A wall with milecastles, turrets, ditches and forts","A line of watchtowers built along the coast","A pair of forts at each end joined by a road"], 1, "The wall was part of a larger frontier system with regular milecastles, turrets, forts, a ditch and the vallum."],
    [1, "Why do historians doubt it was only a defensive barrier?", ["It was built without any soldiers to guard it","It stopped well short of the coast at both ends","It was never connected to any forts or roads","Its many gateways let people cross under supervision"], 3, "Gateways, customs duties and watching the tribes suggest control of movement mattered as much as defence."],
    [2, "Who garrisoned the wall after its construction?", ["Auxiliaries recruited from across the empire","Local Britons drafted from the northern tribes","Citizens of Italy sent out from Rome","The three legions that had built it"], 0, "Auxiliaries from places like Belgium, Spain and Syria served there and became citizens afterwards."],
    [3, "What makes the Vindolanda tablets so valuable?", ["They list every Roman soldier who served in Britain","They are the oldest Roman writing ever found","They preserve ordinary soldiers’ everyday letters","They explain exactly how the wall was designed"], 2, "Handwritten notes about socks, duty rosters and birthday invitations show everyday life on the frontier."],
    [4, "How does Hadrian’s Wall relate to the modern England–Scotland border?", ["It follows the modern border almost exactly","It lies wholly in England, well south of the border","Half of the wall stands inside Scotland today","It was built along the modern border on purpose"], 1, "The border is not the wall, which sits wholly within England."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Hadrian’s Wall isn’t the border with Scotland. So what was it really for?"});

WB.L("medieval-myths", "ancient", "intermediate", "Myths about the Middle Ages",
  "Flat Earth, no baths, and everyone dead by thirty. Much of what people think they know about medieval life was invented or exaggerated centuries later.",
  [
    ["Myth: a flat Earth", "Educated medieval Europeans knew the Earth was a sphere; the flat Earth story mostly arose in the 1800s.",
      "One of the most persistent ideas about the Middle Ages is that people believed the Earth was flat, and that Columbus had to prove otherwise. In fact, educated medieval Europeans knew the Earth was a sphere. Ancient Greek scholars had shown this, and writers such as the English monk Bede in the eighth century took it for granted. A thirteenth-century astronomy textbook, De sphaera, taught a spherical Earth in universities for centuries. Kings were even shown holding an orb, a globe symbolising their rule.\n\nThe argument over Columbus’s voyage was about the size of the Earth, and his critics were right that it was larger than he claimed. The flat Earth myth spread mostly in the 1800s."],
    ["Myth: dead by thirty", "Low average life expectancy reflected high infant mortality, not early old age.",
      "You may have heard that medieval people lived to around thirty. Estimates of average life expectancy at birth in that range are plausible, but averages can mislead. A huge share of children died in infancy or early childhood from disease, and these deaths pulled the average down sharply.\n\nIf a family had one child who died as a baby and another who lived to sixty, their average is about thirty, yet no one died at thirty. People who survived childhood had a reasonable chance of reaching their fifties or sixties, and some lived much longer. Old people were a normal part of medieval communities. Dangers for adults were real, including childbirth, plague, war and accidents, but thirty was not old age."],
    ["Myths: baths and water", "Medieval people washed regularly and drank water, not only beer and wine.",
      "Medieval people did not have modern plumbing, but they were not indifferent to dirt. Washing hands before and after meals was expected etiquette, people washed their faces and bodies with water from basins, and soap was made and sold across Europe. Many towns had public bathhouses, often called stews in England. These became associated with prostitution, and many closed in the sixteenth century, partly because of fears that they spread disease, including syphilis.\n\nThe claim that people drank beer because all water was deadly is also exaggerated. They preferred clean springs, wells and rivers, and towns built fountains and pipes. Medical writings sometimes recommended water. Ale was popular because it was cheap, calorie-rich and enjoyable, and much everyday ale was fairly weak."],
    ["Myths: belts and witches", "Chastity belts and mass witch burnings were mostly inventions or products of later eras, not the Middle Ages.",
      "The image of a crusading knight locking his wife into an iron chastity belt is a favourite of comedies and tourist museums. Historians find almost no reliable evidence for it. The earliest mentions are a handful of fifteenth-century references, several seemingly jokes about jealous husbands. Many belts once displayed in museums are eighteenth- or nineteenth-century creations. Wearing one for long would cause serious infections.\n\nWitch hunts are also often placed in the Middle Ages. In fact, the large-scale hunts mostly came in the early modern period, peaking roughly between 1560 and 1630. Earlier, an early medieval Church text, the Canon Episcopi, treated witches flying at night as an illusion. Belief in organised, devil-worshipping witches grew in the fifteenth century, helped by the Malleus Maleficarum of 1486 and the printing press. Estimates suggest forty to sixty thousand executions, not millions."],
    ["Myth: the Dark Ages", "The ‘Dark Ages’ label is outdated: the medieval centuries brought universities, cathedrals, clocks and more.",
      "In the fourteenth century, the Italian scholar Petrarch helped popularise the idea that the centuries after Rome’s fall were a time of darkness compared with classical antiquity. The label stuck, but most historians now avoid it. The early medieval period does have fewer written sources in some regions, which makes it darker to us in the sense of being harder to see.\n\nBut the medieval centuries also produced the first European universities, such as Bologna, Paris and Oxford, soaring Gothic cathedrals, mechanical clocks, eyeglasses and the spread of windmills. Monasteries copied and preserved many classical texts. Meanwhile, scholars in the Islamic world, Byzantium and China were making major advances. The Middle Ages were not a pause in history."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the real argument about Columbus’s voyage?", ["Whether the Earth was flat or round","Whether the Atlantic had any land at all","The size of the Earth, not its shape","Whether ships could survive a long voyage"], 2, "Educated people knew the Earth was round; Columbus’s critics were right that it was larger than he claimed."],
    [1, "Why does a medieval life expectancy of about thirty mislead?", ["High infant mortality pulled the average down","Most adults died of plague before thirty","Records only covered the poorest people","Adults then aged far faster than today"], 0, "Those who survived childhood could reach their fifties or sixties, so thirty was not old age."],
    [2, "Why was ale so popular in the Middle Ages?", ["Water was too scarce to drink in towns","The Church required it at every meal","Ale was always stronger than wine","It was cheap, nourishing and enjoyable"], 3, "People drank water too, but ale gave cheap energy for hard work and was widely enjoyed."],
    [3, "The large-scale European witch hunts belong mainly to which era?", ["The early Middle Ages, after Rome’s fall","The early modern period, after the Middle Ages","The age of the Crusades in the 1100s","The nineteenth century and the Industrial Age"], 1, "Earlier Church texts were sceptical of witches’ powers; the mass trials came later."],
    [4, "Why do most historians avoid the term ‘Dark Ages’?", ["No written records survive from the whole period","Europe was cut off from changes in other regions","The era produced universities, cathedrals and clocks","It only describes the centuries after the year 1500"], 2, "The label reflects scarce sources in some regions, not an absence of achievement."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Most of what you think you know about medieval life is wrong. Start here."});

WB.L("library-of-alexandria", "ancient", "advanced", "The Library of Alexandria: what really happened",
  "It is the most famous lost library in history, supposedly destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. The truth is slower, stranger and more of a warning.",
  [
    ["A library for the world", "The Ptolemies founded the Library in the early third century BC, inside a royal research institute called the Mouseion.",
      "After Alexander the Great died, his general Ptolemy took Egypt and made Alexandria his capital. Early in the third century BC, Ptolemy I or his son Ptolemy II founded the Mouseion, a temple to the Muses that worked as a research institute, and with it the Library. The Ptolemies wanted Alexandria to outshine Athens as the centre of Greek culture, so they paid scholars to live, work and dine together at royal expense. The aim was bold: collect every important book written in Greek, plus translations of works from other cultures. A later tradition says the Hebrew scriptures were first translated into Greek there, producing the Septuagint."],
    ["Hungry for books", "The Library collected aggressively, but nobody knows its true size, since ancient estimates run from about forty thousand to seven hundred thousand scrolls.",
      "The physician Galen, writing centuries later, told two stories. Ships arriving in the harbour were searched for books, which were copied; the owners received the copies and the Library kept the originals. And Ptolemy III borrowed the official Athenian texts of Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides, left fifteen talents of silver as a deposit, then kept the originals and forfeited the money. These tales may be exaggerated.\n\nThe size is just as uncertain. Ancient writers give figures from about forty thousand to seven hundred thousand scrolls, often long after the fact, and duplicates and multi-scroll works muddle any count. Its main rival was Pergamon, in modern Turkey."],
    ["The scholars", "The Library mattered most for its scholars, who measured the Earth, catalogued literature and edited Homer.",
      "Eratosthenes, one of its chief librarians, estimated the circumference of the Earth by comparing the angle of the Sun's shadow at Alexandria and at Syene, far to the south, on the same day. His result was remarkably close to the true value, though historians debate how close because his units of length are uncertain. The poet Callimachus compiled the Pinakes, a huge catalogue of Greek authors and works, often called an ancestor of the library catalogue. Zenodotus and Aristarchus produced careful editions of Homer by comparing different copies to remove errors. Alexandria also became a great centre of medicine, mathematics and astronomy."],
    ["Caesar's fire", "Julius Caesar's fire in 48 BC burned some books, but sources disagree on what was lost, and scholarship in Alexandria carried on afterwards.",
      "The most famous story of the Library's destruction involves Julius Caesar. In 48 BC, besieged in Alexandria during the civil war involving Cleopatra and her brother, Caesar set fire to ships in the harbour. Several ancient writers say the blaze spread and burned books, and Seneca mentions forty thousand lost. But the sources disagree about what burned. Some historians think the fire mainly destroyed warehouses near the docks holding scrolls awaiting export or cataloguing, not the Library itself. What is clear is that scholarly life continued. The geographer Strabo worked in the city a couple of decades later and wrote about the Mouseion, without mentioning a destroyed library."],
    ["A slow fade", "The Library most likely faded over centuries through purges, war, lost funding and rotting papyrus, not one great fire.",
      "As early as 145 BC, Ptolemy VIII expelled many intellectuals, including the head librarian Aristarchus. Under Roman rule royal patronage vanished, and in the AD 270s fighting under Aurelian devastated the palace quarter where the Library probably stood. A quieter enemy was damp coastal air, which rotted fragile papyrus scrolls unless they were recopied. A library that loses its funding and staff loses its books without any fire.\n\nLater writers blamed convenient villains. The AD 391 destruction of the Serapeum, and the murder of Hypatia in 415, did not destroy the Library. The tale of Caliph Umar burning books in 642 first appears over five centuries later, and most historians reject it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the Ptolemies' ambitious aim for the Library?", ["To store only royal tax records and legal documents","To gather every important Greek book and translations","To gather only works on science and medicine","To keep foreign writings out of Greek scholarship"], 1, "The Library aimed to gather all significant Greek writing and translations, supported by scholars paid to work in the Mouseion."],
    [1, "Why should ancient figures for the Library's size be treated with caution?", ["They were all recorded by Roman tax officials","They count only the books taken from ships","They come from a single source proven forged","They vary widely and were written long after the fact"], 3, "Estimates range from about 40,000 to 700,000 scrolls, and duplicates and multi-scroll works make any count unreliable."],
    [2, "How did Eratosthenes estimate the circumference of the Earth?", ["By comparing Sun shadow angles at Alexandria and Syene","By timing a ship sailing around the Mediterranean","By measuring the height of the Great Pyramid at noon","By counting the steps between Alexandria and Athens"], 0, "Different shadow angles at two distant places on the same day let him calculate the curve of the Earth."],
    [3, "What do historians conclude about Caesar's fire in 48 BC?", ["It destroyed the whole Library and ended scholarship there","It burned only the Serapeum in the south of the city","Sources disagree on what burned, and scholarship continued","It was set deliberately to destroy Greek learning"], 2, "The fire may have hit dockside warehouses, and Strabo later described the Mouseion without noting any ruined library."],
    [4, "What most likely ended the Library?", ["One great fire started by Caliph Umar in 642 AD","A long decline from war, purges and lost funding","A sudden earthquake that buried the whole palace quarter","A royal decree closing it after Hypatia's death in 415"], 1, "Knowledge usually dies through neglect, and the evidence points to centuries of decline rather than one dramatic ending."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The most famous library fire in history may never have destroyed the library."});

